 27200 Data Driven Bioengineering - Transcriptomics Workshop material Welcome Lecturer (theory): Lasse Ebdrup Pedersen (laeb@dtu.dk), Senior Researcher - DTU Bioengineering Responsibles: Juliana Assis, Senior Data Scientist - Biotechnology Research Institute for the Green Transition BRIGHT (jasge@dtu.dk)Sebastian Schulz, Senior Data Scientist - BRIGHT (sebschu@dtu.dk)Alberto Pallej&lt;U+00E0&gt; Caro, Team Lead Data Science Platform - BRIGHT (apca@dtu.dk) Welcome to the Transcriptomics week of 27200 Data-driven Bioengineering! This session is Week 6 of the course (lecture: Thursday, 08 October 2026), part of the omics block. The theme of the week: transcript abundance as a dynamic  but incomplete  regulatory readout. We cover the theory behind RNA sequencing and the full practical workflow  from raw reads to biological interpretation: the RNA-seq workflow and count matrices, normalization, batch effects revisited, PCA, differential expression, and what transcriptomics reflects and misses. The material is designed for people with no or little practical experience in transcriptomic analysis using RNA sequencing (RNA-seq). Objectives Theory on Illumina short-read sequencing technology for transcriptome analysis Theory and practical sessions for RNA-seq data processing using the nf-core/rnaseq pipeline Quality control and exploratory analysis (normalization, PCA, sample correlation) Differential expression analysis with DESeq2  design formulas, shrinkage, multiple testing Enrichment analysis (ORA &amp; GSEA)  gaining biological insights from transcriptomic data Critically assessing what transcript abundance can and cannot tell you about biology Datasets  Staphylococcus aureus  biofilm vs planktonic lifestyle over time (strains USA-100 and USA-500)  Homo sapiens  airway smooth muscle cells treated with dexamethasone (paired donor design) Pick ONE dataset  whichever interests you more, microbial or biomedical  and work through its three notebooks in order (01 quality control  02 differential expression  03 functional enrichment). Depth beats coverage: it is better to interpret one dataset well than to rush through both. If your group finishes early, start on the other dataset. You can also explore the 27200 Data Driven Bioengineering - Transcriptomics repository Draft agenda (4-hour block: ~2 h lecture + ~2 h guided exercises, in groups of 4) Time Session  10:0010:15 Welcome and setup  10:1511:00 Theory: Illumina sequencing, RNA-seq workflow &amp; the nf-core/rnaseq pipeline  11:0011:45 Theory: count matrices, normalization, PCA &amp; differential expression (+ quizzes)  11:4512:00 Break  12:0012:55 Exercises: Quality Control &amp; Exploratory Data Analysis  12:5513:50 Exercises: Differential Expression &amp; Functional Enrichment (ORA/GSEA)  13:5014:00 Wrap-up: key insight + muddy point Material for the workshop is located at: dsp_transcriptomics_27200-Data-driven-bioengineering Below are two setup options for the practical activities: Run the workshop in the cloud (no need to install anything).  Warning: Change the configuration at the Machine type: increase it to 4-core. Launch the app: Run the workshop locally on your machine. To run it on your own machine, install the following packages: # ============================================================ # DSP Transcriptomics Workshop  Package Installation Script # ============================================================ # Run this script ONCE before the workshop to install all # required R packages. Works on macOS, Linux, and Windows. # Tested with R &gt;= 4.3. # ============================================================ ## ---- 0. Helper: install only if missing -------------------- install_if_missing &lt;- function(pkg, installer, ...) { if (!requireNamespace(pkg, quietly = TRUE)) { message(&quot;Installing: &quot;, pkg) installer(pkg, ...) } else { message(&quot;Already installed: &quot;, pkg) } } ## ---- 1. CRAN packages -------------------------------------- cran_pkgs &l